# The icon atlas: one binary object generated by genatlas.py on the host, the
# same shape as the cursors' art. Diffing it means nothing; carrying its
# history inline would mean carrying megabytes per regeneration.

# A pack is an EROFS image with a footer on the end — tens to hundreds of
# megabytes of already-compressed filesystem, and a rebake rewrites the whole
# file. Deltas are the same shape. Neither is diffable and neither belongs in a
# git object.

# NO GIT LFS. The repository used it for 7.4 GB of tarballs and pack blobs, and
# a free account gets 10 GiB of storage and 10 GiB a month of bandwidth shared
# across every repository the account owns — so exceeding it blocked LFS reads
# and a fresh clone could not even check out 7 MB of cursor art.
#
# The heavy artefacts live in releases now, which are 2 GiB per file with no
# cap on total size or bandwidth, and git keeps what IDENTIFIES them: the
# `sha256 =` in each recipe, ports/sources.manifest, and each pack's `C:` in
# PACKAGES. What remains here is small enough to be ordinary git content —
# 7 MB of vendored art and test fixtures — so nothing depends on an LFS budget
# ever again.

